  "text_sanskrit": "क्रोधाद्भवति सम्मोहः सम्मोहात्स्मृतिविभ्रमः । स्मृतिभ्रंशाद् बुद्धिनाशो बुद्धिनाशात्प्रणश्यति ॥ २-६३ ॥",
  "itrans": "krodhAdbhavati sammohaH sammohAtsmR^itivibhramaH . smR^itibhra.nshAd buddhinAsho buddhinAshAtpraNashyati .. 2-63 ..",
  "translation": "From anger results the wrong wish; from (this) wrong wish, forgetfulness (of Law); from (this) forgetfulness, loss of judgement; from the loss of judgement, he perishes. (63) \nV. When the accomplishment of a desire is checked by anything, anger bursts forth. Sammoha or the wrong wish is the inclination to do what ought not to be done: it may be mistaking the unrighteous conduct of appropriating another's wealth, &c, to be perfectly righteous. Front such a wrong tendency and such unrighteous activities the memory of Laws prohibiting them becomes impaired and the very sacred rules once so clearly understood by himself may now give him a very different meaning, why, they may seem to sanction to his wrong ways. Then gradually comes the loss of judgement which allows him unrestrained to be more and more wicked things. When he is thus concentrated in evil, He must purely go to hell and suffer the consequences. The significance of this genesis of passions, especially of Likes and Dislikes is: When it is known that they lead, stop by step, to hell and other sufferings, there comes the wish to get rid of them; he first ceases to muse on the objects of senses and then gradually checks the growth of those passions and eventually destroys their root. :(62 & 63.) \nA. Like and Dislike may go, os has been shown above. But what effect does that leave behind? It is the control of the senses, the Lord says; He also gives the effect of this control in the following.",
